  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nTonight - Tuesday: Showers and Storms Likely\n\nPrecipitation chances will be on the rise overnight and into Tuesday \nas a pronounced 500mb trough pushes through south-central Canada \ntonight and into Tuesday. As this occurs, a surface low pressure \ncenter with a trailing front will move through the local area and \nincrease precipitation chances. In addition, a fairly profound axis \nof 850mb moisture transport pushes through the region advecting \nprecipitable waters up to around 2 inches into the region as shown \nacross deterministic guidance. As a result, the recent HREF has \nfairly high probabilities (50-70%) for rainfall amounts over 0.5\" \nacross portions of north-central WI and northeast IA. This is due to \nthe fact that the initial round of precipitation is favored \ngenerally north of I-94 with the initial front and a second \npotentially further where the front remains slower. As far as \nsevere weather potential is concerned, confidence is fairly low \nin the potential as the better instability axis is generally \ncollocated with the 850mb moisture transport axis which remains \nlargely west of the local area. In addition, the speed max with \nthe with the upper-level jet streak remains well to the west of \nthe local area. Consequently, much of the deep-layer shear is \nabsent with bulk shear values generally remaining under 30 kts \nbased on RAP soundings. However, given the marginal DCAPE up to \n900 J/kg along the front coupled with weak instability, cannot \nrule out some strong to marginally severe storms capable of \nproducing strong wind gusts. Generally the aforementioned front \nexits the region by Wednesday morning and into the afternoon. \n\nWednesday - Thursday: Drier, Near-Normal Temperatures\n\nAs we head into Wednesday and Thursday, northwesterly flow builds \ninto the region aloft with surface high pressure moving into the \nregion in the wake of the aforementioned front. Consequently, \nexpecting relatively precipitation free conditions during this \nperiod of the forecast. However, in spite of the frontal passage, \nnot expecting temperatures to budge much with zonal flow and upper-\nlevel ridging attempting to build into the region. As a result, NBM \ngenerally keeps highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s through the end \nof the work week. \n\nFriday - This Weekend: Rain Chances, Potentially Cooling Down \n\nBy the time late Friday and the weekend rolls around, a pronounced \nupper-level trough will move through the Upper-Midwest bringing \nadditional potential for precipitation Friday night and into \nSaturday. Ensemble guidance is fairly enthusiastic about some \nprecipitation during this time frame as the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has fairly high probabilities (50-80%) \nfor measurable precipitation during this timeframe. Overall not \nimpressed with the severe potential with this round of precipitation \nas the timing is not overly favorable with the precipitation moving \nin overnight and instability being relatively lackluster. However, \ngiven some AI/ML guidance shows some small probabilities (~5% \nchance) Friday night and into Saturday for severe weather, cannot \nrule it out if the timing of this system ends up being slightly \nfaster. Otherwise, in the wake of this trough passing through the \nregion, the potential exists for northwesterly flow to entrench \nitself over the region resulting in some cooler temperatures late \ninto the weekend. However, given the wide range in high temperatures \nby Sunday in the NBM with the 10-90th percentile ranging from the \nmiddle 60s to lower 80s, confidence remains fairly low in exactly \nhow much modification to our summertime airmass this troughing \nregime will do.",
